Animal telepathy - foreknowledge - mysterious orientation

Animals as clairvoyants and prophets Telepathy, orientation, diagnosis, foreknowledge: The fantastic abilities of animals Amazing abilities





Herminia Denot, a student who grew up on an Argentinian ranch, and her horse “Pampero” were an inseparable pair, even when Herminia had to leave her favorite animal to go to high school in Buenos Aires and could only return during the holidays.


A common separation story like millions of others, were it not for the astonishing observations of the caring gaucho and the family during Herminia's visits. They observed that Pampero was always acting crazy days before Herminia's return and galloping around the paddock neighing.

On the day of arrival, Pampero stood at the gate and waited. If Herminia came by train, he stood in the north and looked towards the station; if her parents picked her up in the car, he waited in the southeast, towards the road. -

We humans have very different relationships with animals. At the bottom end of the scale is the cruel "consumption" of millions of test animals in research laboratories, and mass animal husbandry in meat factories really cannot be seen as an exemplary example of humane treatment of animals.

At the other end of the scale, however, there are also millions of pets that are loved very much by their owners, sometimes more than family members. And it is not uncommon for pets to be more than good-natured, loving and loyal companions, as they have certain abilities that go far beyond those of most people and which, if we were to use them, would be extremely valuable and helpful.

For example, they have

the ability to telepathically sense when a loved one is about to return home;

the ability to be connected to people over thousands of kilometers and to sense how they are doing;

an incredible sense of direction that allows an animal to find its home, nesting or spawning ground, or even "its" human over any distance and thus rescue them from distress;

a diagnostic ability that allows dogs in particular to detect certain illnesses, such as certain types of cancer, with high accuracy;

an early detection of fainting and epileptic attacks, which enables animals to protect the people concerned from serious injury or even death;

- the most extraordinary ability is the ability to know hours or even days in advance that accidents or serious natural disasters such as earthquakes or tsunamis are about to occur, which could actually save millions of human lives.

The amazing abilities of animals have always attracted the attention of many people, so that reports about them have been handed down for centuries, and in some cases for millennia - especially when animals have rescued people from distress or protected them from serious accidents. However, in relation to the importance and fascination of these phenomena, there is surprisingly little serious and systematic research into them.

The English biologist Rupert Sheldrake spent years collecting the most comprehensive case studies in a database and systematically evaluating them (see GralsWelt interview in issue 50). The majority of the following case descriptions come from his highly recommended book "The Seventh Sense of Animals". It explains in detail why most amazing events cannot be explained by animals' perceptions using the usual five senses!

Sheldrake can be considered one of the few scientists who does not simply ignore or deny amazing phenomena such as telepathy, but tries to get closer to them and explain them. He coined the term "morphic fields" for the proven connection between living beings that cannot be explained by even the most acute sense, but rather suggests a kind of invisible "rubber band": a kind of shape-forming energetic field that, for example, also holds groups of animals together in a mysterious way, is infinitely stretchable and thus enables individual animals to find their way back to the group over great distances.

The connection between an animal and a human and vice versa is said to take place in this way, whereby one thing is crucial for the degree of attraction: it depends on the strength of the sympathy , in other words the love, that an animal feels for its owner. Likewise, a strong aversion to a person or to undesirable treatment can be the decisive factor for a corresponding rejection or flight reaction, which can set in several minutes before the person arrives.

Sheldrake's assumption that morphic, i.e. shape-forming fields exist that cannot be measured or proven is quite bold in today's scientific community, since it takes him into terrain that is considered vulnerable. As such, he continues to expose himself to more or less strong hostility from scientific colleagues who are not prepared to look for solutions to observable phenomena that cannot be measured and therefore deny them.

But even with this bold approach in mind, the question arises whether, from a holistic perspective, taking subtle levels and realities into account, perhaps even simpler explanations for the astonishing observations might not be found.

But after so much theory, let's now turn to the fascinating, often heart-warming practice and look at some case studies!


Animals as harbingers of the arrival of their owners

First, we will look at cases where pets, mostly dogs and cats, but also parrots, budgies, monkeys, etc., seem to inexplicably know when their owner is coming home. One case, also documented by Austrian television (ORF) in 1994, involves the Englishwoman Pam Smart and her terrier crossbreed named Jaytee.

Mrs. Smart worked as a secretary and continued to live with her parents. Their parents noticed right from the start that Jaytee left his usual place almost every day at 4.30 p.m. to wait for Pam at the patio door, about 45 to 60 minutes before she arrived home. It was therefore reasonable to assume that dogs had a very precise sense of time.

Things got strange when Pam Smart lost her job in 1993 and then always came home at the most irregular times. No matter what reason she was somewhere, Jaytee always reacted by walking expectantly to the patio door exactly when Pam Smart decided to come home: on foot, by car, by taxi, whether near or far away, with or against the wind, it didn't matter.

In the ORF scientific documentary, one camera was installed to observe Jaytee, the other ran synchronously and accompanied Pam Smart, who had to drive home when her pager beeped - she herself did not know when beforehand. And so you could watch Pam Smart on the left half of the screen and Jaytee the dog on the right, reacting synchronously to Mrs Smart's intention - without any normal means of communication!

Jaytee rarely did not react; usually when he was ill or distracted by a female dog in heat from the neighborhood. -

There is a case that is both funny and astonishing regarding a female spaniel that belonged to the mother-in-law of a veterinarian. Here is the vet's description:

"The dog always knew whether I was visiting in private or as a vet. She would jump up and howl with joy when I was visiting in private, but when I was visiting in a veterinary capacity, she would hide behind the boiler. I couldn't figure out how she knew I was visiting in a veterinary capacity - but she decided to hide before I entered the house anyway. And every time she was right. I actually came to visit quite often, to stop by or do all sorts of things, but I rarely came to visit as a vet. And I didn't just make my vet visits because the dog was sick - sometimes they could be routine matters. But the dog always knew when I was on duty and when I wasn't."

Also interesting are cases where pet owners change their intentions during the journey home, for example to stop off for some shopping. The animals react to this too, for example with a certain amount of irritation - or they go back to their usual place until the person they are attached to has finished and decides to go home again.

There are many other touching descriptions in which time and distance play no role. Be it the beloved horse "Pampero" mentioned at the beginning, who senses Herminia's anticipation days in advance and reciprocates it, be it the dog of a stewardess who is just boarding the plane home thousands of kilometers away, or many other cases: the reaction is there!

But how can something like this be explained when references to bodily functions, such as extremely heightened senses, are definitely unsatisfactory as an explanation? Aren't we dealing here with beautiful, clear and mostly pleasing references to the existence of phenomena in a subtle world?

The key to this seems to me to lie in the lectures on thought and feeling forms in the work "In the Light of Truth". There it is explained that we humans create corresponding forms for everything we think and feel, which are energetically highly effective . These forms are also not tied to distance.

So if there is a "connection", a "pull" between people or between people and animals - and what bond could be more intense than that of love - then these thought and feeling forms immediately form a bridge. And it seems that animals generally react more strongly to these influences than we humans do. However, Rupert Sheldrake has also recorded similar cases among people, particularly among mothers and their children.


The empathy and compassion of animals

One ability with which animals can support us humans in a tremendous way is their empathy. For millions of people, beloved animals are a great help in coping better with loneliness, illness or grief, and sociological studies have repeatedly shown that reference groups of people with animals are healthier and more balanced than those without animals.

The help of animals can go a long way! Here is an impressive example:

"When her marital problems became too much for her, a woman in northern England decided to take her own life. She left her dog and cats 'sleeping peacefully in front of the fire' and went into the kitchen to get a glass of water and a bottle of paracetamol tablets. Suddenly William, her beloved English Springer Spaniel, jumped up, ran towards her and did something he had never done in all his 15 years of life: 'He growled! His lips were completely retracted so that he was almost unrecognisable,' she reported. 'Horrified, I screwed the bottle back on. I was really scared of the dog, went back into the room and sat on the sofa. William ran after me, jumped on me and started licking my face like mad, his whole body shaking.'"

Here is the case of a dog that shows that knowing the well-being of its caregiver can also bridge great distances, in this case about 10,000 kilometers:

"My son was very close to our West Highland Terrier. [...] In April 1982, his ship, the HMS Coventry, was sent to the Falkland Islands. On May 25, in the early evening, the dog jumped onto my lap, shaking and whining. When my husband came home, I said, 'I don't know what's wrong with her - she's been like this for over half an hour. She won't get off my lap.' On the nine o'clock news we heard that a 'Type 42' ship had been sunk, and that's when we knew it was the HMS Coventry, although the name was not announced until the next day. Our son was among the missing. Our little dog was consumed with grief and died after a few months.” (Iris Hall, Cowley near Oxford)

But this bond can also work the other way round. For example, a horse owner woke up one night and was very worried about his favourite mare. He equipped himself with a flashlight and wire cutters, set off in the pitch darkness and intuitively quickly found the horse that was caught in the barbed wire.

In all of these cases, it can be assumed that a subtle bond of strong affection exists between the human spirit and the animal's soul, a kind of radiation connection that can serve as an “information conductor” with regard to mutual well-being and signals when more or less drastic things are happening.


Telepathy and telephony

Wonderful devices for proving the telepathic abilities of animals are telephones, at least the older ones without a display and with a single ring tone. With these telephones, no one can know who is calling - or can they?

There are numerous cases of cats in particular, but also dogs, in which animals only respond to calls from their caregivers, regardless of the time, while completely ignoring calls from other people! Rupert Sheldrake describes a case in which a cat was particularly sensitive to phone calls from an owner whom it was particularly fond of. As soon as the owner called, it ran to the phone and sometimes managed to throw the receiver off the hook to greet it with a happy meow. It was completely uninterested in any other caller.

In the case of a dog called Jack, the reaction started even before the phone rang:

"About ten minutes before this call was made, the dog sat by the phone until it rang. On the evenings when my father didn't have to call, the dog didn't move at all and stayed in its basket. Furthermore, he did not bother with any other calls at any other time.” (Mr. S. Waller)

Many people also have the ability to guess certain callers, but usually in a much weaker and less reliable form. -

In these cases too, it is obvious to assume that the animals pick up the intentions of their owners via thought and sensation forms, i.e. again via the subtle radiation path.


“Navigators” thousands of years old - mysterious sense of orientation

Who has not come across a small or large truck on the motorway bearing the logo of one of the numerous racing pigeon clubs! It has long been a popular hobby there to take racing pigeons distances of 100 to a whopping 1,100 kilometers away from their home dovecote, release them there and then record when they have found their way back home, completely on their own.

A report might look like this: "Viernheim: 1,843 pigeons from the flight association were launched at 10:05 a.m. in changing weather conditions. The first prize pigeon reached a speed of 1,507 meters per minute. A very good flight with a flight time of just 11 minutes and no losses."

Prize-winning pigeons fly up to 1,100 kilometers without a break and arrive home after about 12 hours of flying - an incredible achievement with an average speed of about 90 km/h, which also proves one thing: there is no time to search! The pigeons fly straight home from places that are completely unknown to them and are constantly changing.

Experiments in which the pigeons were temporarily blinded by putting frosted glass lenses on them showed that this did not affect their ability to find their home loft at all. However, the final approach to landing caused major problems under these conditions - the pigeons collided with trees or fences, for example. They obviously need visual contact for the last few meters.

Albatrosses that were brought to other continents found their nesting holes accurately over distances of 5,000 to 6,500 kilometers and across oceans within just two to four weeks.

Attempts are repeatedly made to explain these phenomena by orienting themselves using the sun, the stars or the earth's magnetic field, but in the vast majority of cases this is not convincing, as the birds can find their route accurately even at night or when the sky is overcast. With regard to the magnetic field, the earth's magnetic poles could of course be identified, assuming such an enigmatic magnetic sense. But none of this is of any use if the geographical coordinates of the animal are not known. It would then only know where north or south is, but not where it is at the moment! Let us remember that reliable navigation on the world's oceans was a great challenge for us humans until relatively recently!

Even sea creatures such as turtles can find their way back to their spawning grounds despite travelling thousands of kilometres in constantly changing ocean currents.

Research is still being carried out into how all this might work. However, a satisfactory explanation based on a purely materialistic approach without taking the subtle worlds into account is probably not possible!

In this regard, it is worth considering the reference in the work "In the Light of Truth" that nature is also populated by conscious natural beings who are generally invisible to us humans. On this basis, wouldn't one of the simplest explanations for the phenomenal orientation ability of many animals be found: nature beings can be seen by the animals and show the birds, turtles, etc. the right, shortest or best route! It cannot be ruled out that nature beings are actually able to create a kind of energetic field or a subtle radiation connection to the target, which the animals then simply follow. Such an ordered energetic and shape-forming, "morphic" field could at least partially explain

why, for example, flocks of birds move completely synchronously, in a mysterious wave that moves through the entire flock in a fraction of a second;

- why blind termites are able to build an incredibly complex structure independently of one another in a perfect division of labour;

- what allows schools of fish with thousands of small individual fish to form a larger hole in a fraction of a second when attacked by a predatory fish - perfectly coordinated - through which the attacker then breaks through without success.


Dogs as prophets and diagnosticians

The ability of dogs in particular to anticipate their owners' epileptic fits or dangerously low blood sugar levels and then warn them is astonishing. Even if it is sometimes only a few minutes, this is usually enough for the epileptic or diabetic to protect themselves or get to safety in time. Studies on the warning behaviour of dogs were carried out by the British veterinarian Andrew Edney, and Rupert Sheldrake also mentions very interesting cases.

For example, the prescient abilities of a rescue dog called Rupert, owned by Antonia Brown-Griffin from Kent, who suffers up to twelve severe epileptic seizures a week, are both fantastic and touching:

"He can sense up to fifty minutes in advance that I am about to have a seizure and taps me twice with his paw, which gives me time to get to safety somewhere. He can also press a button on my phone and bark when someone answers the phone to call for help, and if he thinks I am about to have a seizure while I am in the bath, he pulls the plug out. I simply cannot imagine life without him."

In such cases, it is conceivable that the dogs are able to react to the slightest disturbances in their owner's energy balance, which we humans can also perceive in a kind of change in the aura of our fellow human beings. This cannot be ruled out if there is close personal contact with the owner. However, it becomes puzzling when the animal is in the next room, perhaps sleeping, and comes shooting out of there at the time of the warning. In this case, an explanation involving contact with (natural) beings is certainly more likely. -

Only one other form of animal diagnosis can be explained by the physical senses, even if it is also astonishing: some dogs have a pronounced ability to diagnose certain types of cancer, such as skin or lung cancer, with a high accuracy rate! For lung cancer, the accuracy rate is an incredible 99 percent, and for breast cancer, 88 percent, significantly higher than the accuracy rate of conventional mammography. The dogs trained in this way then sniff the patient's breath and the relevant skin tissue and sit down if they can detect cancer.

This can be explained by the extraordinary sensitivity of the dog's nose, which, with 220 million olfactory cells, is far more sensitive than that of humans (5 million).

Such a gentle diagnosis or confirmation of the diagnosis would be a great help. However, this simple approach has not yet been taken in clinics, at least not on a large scale.



Cats also apparently have a “seventh sense”.

  

Warning of accidents and disasters For a long time now there have been reports of animals stopping people or making them turn around through extremely unusual behavior, thus saving them from injury or even death. Here too we are dealing with situations that cannot be explained conclusively with normal, even the most acute senses, because some of the events perceived are in the future, such as traffic accidents or falling trees, which are registered minutes beforehand by dogs or cats traveling in the car due to unusual behavior, which is relatively loud and isolated from external influences. Animals have also warned of a landslide that turned a mountain road into a gaping abyss after a bend, and other similar events. There are reports from the Second World War in which animals warned of enemy bombing raids and were able to distinguish precisely between the harmless flight movements of their own air force and those of the enemy attackers. Even more striking are the premonitions of animals that were able to anticipate the Germans' insidious V2 rocket attacks, even though these flew at almost three times the speed of sound, sometimes at night, and were therefore impossible to perceive with normal senses.








Snakes and toads as lifesavers


Snakes are excellent earthquake warning devices.


Countless human lives could be saved by what is perhaps the most valuable ability of animals: the premonition of major natural disasters, which GralsWelt recently reported on in the article "Why?" (issue 58) in connection with the earthquake in Haiti (January 2010). There we mentioned extremely conspicuous animal behavior not only during this earthquake, but also before the devastating tsunami in December 2006 and other earthquakes in China, where in one case the city of Haicheng was actually evacuated in early February 1975, saving the lives of many thousands of people.

Toads and snakes in particular are often excellent earthquake warning devices, often reacting many days in advance. On March 31, 2010, the “Welt-online” newspaper ran the headline: “Toads are better than the best seismographs” and continued: “The author of the study, biologist Rachel Grant from the British Open University, traveled to Lake San Ruffino, 74 kilometers north of L'Aquila, ten days before the earthquake to conduct scientific observations of toads. While on March 28 [2009] more than 90 male toads had gathered around the lake for spawning, two days later the number dropped rapidly. On April 1, five days before the earthquake, 96 percent of the male toads had disappeared.

A few days after the earthquake on April 6, dozens of common toads returned to the lake for the full moon, a peak of the mating season. However, the number was 50 to 80 percent lower than in previous years and then immediately dropped again.

Only two days after the last major aftershock did the number of toads observed increase again significantly. In addition, three days before the earthquake, all toad pairs had disappeared. Between the earthquake and the last major aftershock, no fresh toad spawn was discovered.

According to Grant, the toads' behavior is extremely unusual: Normally, male toads would not leave a certain area until the end of the spawning season." And so there are numerous reports that wild animals always seek safety from natural disasters, so that, for example, the beach after the severe tsunami in 2006 was littered with human corpses, but no animal carcasses were found anywhere.


